<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 5</p>
  <h1>Comparatives and superlatives</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Make the scale and the comparison set clear: <span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than Maple. Maple is the largest of the three rooms. Pine is as quiet as Maple.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">scale · reference · comparison set</span>
    <span class="tag">-er / -est · more / most · as...as</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Read one room table before making comparisons.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Separate a reference comparison from an extreme in a set.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control form, spelling, equality, degree, and articles.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Recommend a fictional event room from stated criteria.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can compare two values, identify an extreme within a defined set, express equal or unequal degree, and justify a recommendation with exact evidence.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved base adjectives, plural be agreement, and a neutral future forecast.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the stated description, subject, or future stance.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quiet">quiet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quietly">quietly</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be">will be</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="was">was</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The room has very little noise, so the base adjective is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quiet" data-hint="After be, use the adjective quiet to describe the room." data-fix="The room is quiet." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The plural subject rooms takes plural present be: The rooms <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="Rooms is plural, so use are." data-fix="The rooms are available." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> available.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The coordinator gives a neutral belief about tomorrow: I think the event <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be" data-hint="I think frames a neutral future prediction with will." data-fix="I think the event will be busy." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> busy.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice the evidence before the grammar</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Three rooms at Harbor Center</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>room</th><th>area</th><th>hourly price</th><th>background noise</th><th>chairs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Pine</b></td><td>30 m²</td><td>$40</td><td>35 dB</td><td>24</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Cedar</b></td><td>42 m²</td><td>$55</td><td>30 dB</td><td>32</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Maple</b></td><td>50 m²</td><td>$70</td><td>35 dB</td><td>40</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><span class="ex">Cedar is larger than Pine. Maple is the largest of the three rooms. Pine is as quiet as Maple. Cedar is the quietest room in this set.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which sentence compares one room with a reference room?</li>
      <li>Which sentences locate an extreme inside the three-room set?</li>
      <li>Which two rooms occupy the same point on the noise scale?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the data to identify a comparative, superlative, equality frame, reference word, article, and defined set.">
    <h3>Discovery check: connect grammar to the table</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Comparison discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="larger">larger</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="largest">largest</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="as quiet as">as quiet as</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="than">than</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the">the</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="of the three rooms">of the three rooms</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Cedar has 42 m² and Pine has 30 m²: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="larger" data-hint="The sentence compares Cedar’s area with Pine’s area." data-fix="Cedar is larger than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> than Pine.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Maple has more area than both other rooms: Maple is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="largest" data-hint="Maple occupies the high extreme for area in the three-room set." data-fix="Maple is the largest of the three rooms." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> of the three rooms.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Pine and Maple both measure 35 dB: Pine is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="as quiet as" data-hint="Equal values use as plus the base adjective plus as." data-fix="Pine is as quiet as Maple." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Maple.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>To name Pine as Cedar’s reference, use the comparison word: Cedar is quieter <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="than" data-hint="Than introduces the standard used for the comparison." data-fix="Cedar is quieter than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Pine.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>No possessive comes before this superlative, so use the usual article: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the" data-hint="An adjective superlative normally uses the when no possessive determiner replaces it." data-fix="Cedar is the quietest room." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> quietest room.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The claim needs a defined comparison set: Maple is the most expensive <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="of the three rooms" data-hint="Of the three rooms tells the listener exactly which options belong to the set." data-fix="Maple is the most expensive of the three rooms." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: choose a scale, reference, and set</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A comparative locates one value relative to another reference on the same scale: <span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than Maple</span>. The reference can be one item, a previous situation, or a group: <span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than the other rooms</span>. A comparative does not require exactly two physical things.</p>
  <p>A superlative locates an extreme inside a defined set: <span class="ex">Cedar is the quietest of the three rooms</span>. The set matters. Cedar may be the quietest in this list without being the quietest room in the entire building.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning job</th><th>frame</th><th>evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>relative difference</td><td><span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than Maple.</span></td><td>30 dB compared with 35 dB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>extreme in a set</td><td><span class="ex">Cedar is the quietest of the three.</span></td><td>30 dB is the lowest value in this set</td></tr>
      <tr><td>equal degree</td><td><span class="ex">Pine is as quiet as Maple.</span></td><td>both measure 35 dB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>unequal degree</td><td><span class="ex">Pine is not as large as Cedar.</span></td><td>30 m² does not reach 42 m²</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">A superlative does not guarantee one unique winner. Two items can share an extreme: if Pine and Maple both cost $40, they are the joint cheapest rooms. Say <span class="ex">one of the cheapest rooms</span> when an item belongs to a top group.</p>

  <h2>Build the form without a false syllable shortcut</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>adjective pattern</th><th>comparative</th><th>superlative</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>most one-syllable: small, fast</td><td>smaller, faster</td><td>the smallest, the fastest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>final silent e: large, nice</td><td>larger, nicer</td><td>the largest, the nicest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>consonant + y: busy, happy</td><td>busier, happier</td><td>the busiest, the happiest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>some common two-syllable: simple, quiet, narrow</td><td>simpler, quieter, narrower</td><td>the simplest, the quietest, the narrowest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>most other two-syllable and longer: useful, modern, comfortable</td><td>more useful, more modern, more comfortable</td><td>the most useful, the most modern, the most comfortable</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Two-syllable adjectives do not follow one perfect mechanical rule. Endings such as <span class="ex">-y</span>, <span class="ex">-er</span>, <span class="ex">-ow</span>, and syllabic <span class="ex">-le</span> often take <span class="ex">-er / -est</span>, but usage varies. Learn the common form with the adjective. Some forms coexist, such as <span class="ex">cleverer</span> and <span class="ex">more clever</span>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Precise spelling conditions</h3>
    <p>Add only <span class="ex">-r / -st</span> after final silent <span class="ex">e</span>: <span class="ex">large → larger → largest</span>. Change consonant plus <span class="ex">y</span> to <span class="ex">i</span>: <span class="ex">busy → busier → busiest</span>, but keep the <span class="ex">y</span> after a vowel in <span class="ex">gray → grayer → grayest</span>.</p>
    <p>Double a final consonant in a one-syllable consonant-vowel-consonant spelling with a short stressed vowel: <span class="ex">big → bigger</span>, <span class="ex">hot → hottest</span>. Do not double final <span class="ex">w</span>, <span class="ex">x</span>, or <span class="ex">y</span>: <span class="ex">newer, laxer, grayer</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled one-syllable, silent-e, consonant-doubling, final-y, common two-syllable, longer, and irregular forms.">
    <h3>Drill 1: build the form from the exact condition</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Comparative and superlative form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="faster">faster</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="largest">largest</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="bigger">bigger</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="busiest">busiest</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quieter">quieter</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="more useful">more useful</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="better">better</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="worst">worst</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Route A takes 20 minutes and Route B takes 30: Route A is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="faster" data-hint="The one-syllable adjective fast normally adds -er." data-fix="Route A is faster than Route B." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> than Route B.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Maple has the greatest area in the set: It is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="largest" data-hint="Large already ends in silent e, so add only -st for the superlative." data-fix="Maple is the largest room in the set." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> room in the set.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Cedar has 42 m² and Pine has 30 m²: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="bigger" data-hint="Big has a short stressed vowel in a one-syllable consonant-vowel-consonant pattern, so double g." data-fix="Cedar is bigger than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> than Pine.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The center serves more visitors on Saturday than on every other day: Saturday is its <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="busiest" data-hint="Busy ends in consonant plus y, so change y to i before -est." data-fix="Saturday is the center’s busiest day." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> day.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Cedar measures 30 dB and Maple 35 dB: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quieter" data-hint="Quiet is a common two-syllable adjective that normally takes -er." data-fix="Cedar is quieter than Maple." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> than Maple.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Room B has a projector and Room A does not, so B is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="more useful" data-hint="Useful normally forms its comparative with more." data-fix="Room B is more useful for a presentation than Room A." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> for a presentation.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Cedar scores 5 out of 5 and Pine scores 3 out of 5 for comfort: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="better" data-hint="Good has the irregular comparative better." data-fix="Cedar is better for comfort than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> for comfort.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Route C has the lowest rating of all three routes: It is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="worst" data-hint="Bad has the irregular superlative worst." data-fix="Route C is the worst of the three routes by rating." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> of the three by rating.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Irregulars, articles, and comparison frames</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>base</th><th>comparative</th><th>superlative</th><th>note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>good</td><td>better</td><td>the best</td><td>not gooder or more good</td></tr>
      <tr><td>bad</td><td>worse</td><td>the worst</td><td>not badder or more bad</td></tr>
      <tr><td>far</td><td>farther / further</td><td>the farthest / furthest</td><td>both occur for distance; further also means additional</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Current U.S. English uses both <span class="ex">farther</span> and <span class="ex">further</span> for physical distance. <span class="ex">Farther</span> makes the physical-distance meaning especially clear; <span class="ex">further</span> is also the normal choice for abstract or additional extent: <span class="ex">further information</span>.</p>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">the</span> before an adjective superlative when no determiner replaces it: <span class="ex">the largest room</span>. A possessive already fills that position: <span class="ex">our largest room</span>, not <span class="ex">our the largest room</span>. A superlative reference can be omitted when the set is already clear: <span class="ex">Which room is cheapest?</span></p>

  <h2>Equal, unequal, and measured differences</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use base adjective inside <span class="ex">as + adjective + as</span> for equal degree: <span class="ex">Pine is as quiet as Maple</span>. Use <span class="ex">not as...as</span> when the first item does not reach the same degree: <span class="ex">Pine is not as large as Cedar</span>.</p>
  <p>The meaning follows the adjective’s scale. <span class="ex">The delay was not as bad as yesterday’s</span> means today’s delay had a lower degree of badness, so it was better. Do not replace this meaning with a mechanical rule that always says “less” without checking the adjective.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>degree message</th><th>natural frame</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>large difference</td><td>much / far / a lot + comparative</td><td><span class="ex">Maple is much larger than Pine.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>small difference</td><td>a little / slightly + comparative</td><td><span class="ex">Cedar is a little more expensive than Pine.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>exact difference</td><td>amount + comparative</td><td><span class="ex">Cedar is $15 more expensive than Pine.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>equal multiple</td><td>twice as + adjective + as</td><td><span class="ex">Room X is twice as large as Room Y.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Use one comparative marker: <span class="ex">larger</span> or <span class="ex">more comfortable</span>, not <span class="ex">more larger</span>. Degree modifiers such as <span class="ex">much better</span> are different: <span class="ex">better</span> marks the comparison, and <span class="ex">much</span> describes its size. Do not say <span class="ex">very bigger</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled equality, unequal degree, large and exact differences, a possessive superlative, and membership in a tied top group.">
    <h3>Drill 2: complete the comparison frame</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Comparison frame choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="as quiet as">as quiet as</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="not as large as">not as large as</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much larger than">much larger than</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="$15 more expensive than">$15 more expensive than</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="our largest room">our largest room</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="one of the cheapest rooms">one of the cheapest rooms</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Pine and Maple both measure 35 dB: Pine is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="as quiet as" data-hint="Equal noise values use as plus base adjective quiet plus as." data-fix="Pine is as quiet as Maple." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Maple.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Pine has 30 m² and Cedar has 42 m², so Pine is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="not as large as" data-hint="Pine does not reach Cedar’s area, so use not as large as." data-fix="Pine is not as large as Cedar." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Cedar.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Maple has 50 m² and Pine has 30 m², a clearly substantial difference: Maple is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much larger than" data-hint="Much can emphasize a large difference before the comparative larger." data-fix="Maple is much larger than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Pine.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Cedar costs $55 and Pine costs $40 per hour: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="$15 more expensive than" data-hint="The prices provide an exact fifteen-dollar comparative difference." data-fix="Cedar is $15 more expensive than Pine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Pine.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The center owns the room, so its possessive replaces <span class="ex">the</span>: Maple is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="our largest room" data-hint="Our already determines the noun phrase, so do not add the before largest." data-fix="Maple is our largest room." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Pine and Oak share the building’s lowest hourly price: Pine is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="one of the cheapest rooms" data-hint="One of the plus plural noun includes Pine in a tied extreme group." data-fix="Pine is one of the cheapest rooms in the building." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> in the building.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: stress the scale, lighten the frame</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In relaxed U.S. English, <span class="ex">than</span> is often weak /ðən/, while the compared items and adjective root carry stronger beats: <span class="ex"><strong>CE</strong>dar is <strong>QUI</strong>eter than <strong>MA</strong>ple.</span> The <span class="ex">-er</span> ending is normally weak. In words such as <span class="ex">better</span> and <span class="ex">hotter</span>, the middle <span class="ex">t</span> often sounds like the quick flap heard in U.S. <span class="ex">water</span>.</p>
  <p>The superlative ending <span class="ex">-est</span> adds a weak syllable after many roots: <span class="ex">fastest</span>, <span class="ex">quietest</span>. Keep <span class="ex">the</span> light unless it is contrastive. In <span class="ex">as <strong>QUIET</strong> as</span>, both instances of <span class="ex">as</span> are usually weak and the adjective holds the main stress.</p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read comparative reference, superlative set, equality, unequal degree, exact difference, and tied-extreme listening check">
    <h2>Listen: find the scale and set</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once naturally and once more slowly. Listen for weak frame words, strong adjective roots, and the evidence relationship. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard a reference comparison, defined superlative set, equality, unequal degree, exact difference, and tied extreme.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="quiz-list">
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Maple is the reference" data-hint="Than Maple names the standard used to locate Cedar on the noise scale." data-fix="Cedar is quieter than Maple, so Maple is the reference."><p class="quiz-question">1. What is Cedar compared with in Clip 1?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Maple is the reference">Maple is the reference</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine is the reference">Pine is the reference</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the whole building is the reference">the whole building is the reference</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="the three listed rooms" data-hint="Of the three rooms defines the complete comparison set." data-fix="Maple is the largest of the three listed rooms."><p class="quiz-question">2. What comparison set limits the claim in Clip 2?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the three listed rooms">the three listed rooms</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="every room in the city">every room in the city</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="only Pine and Cedar">only Pine and Cedar</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Pine and Maple have equal noise levels" data-hint="As quiet as places both rooms at the same point on the stated scale." data-fix="Pine is as quiet as Maple, so their noise levels are equal."><p class="quiz-question">3. What equality does Clip 3 express?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine and Maple have equal noise levels">Pine and Maple have equal noise levels</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine is quieter than Maple">Pine is quieter than Maple</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Maple is quieter than Cedar">Maple is quieter than Cedar</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Pine has less area than Cedar" data-hint="Not as large as means Pine does not reach Cedar’s area." data-fix="Pine is not as large as Cedar, so Pine has less area."><p class="quiz-question">4. What does Clip 4 establish?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine has less area than Cedar">Pine has less area than Cedar</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine and Cedar have equal area">Pine and Cedar have equal area</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine has more area than Cedar">Pine has more area than Cedar</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="$15 per hour" data-hint="The amount before more expensive gives the exact price difference." data-fix="Cedar is $15 more expensive per hour than Pine."><p class="quiz-question">5. What exact difference does Clip 5 give?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="$15 per hour">$15 per hour</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="$40 per hour">$40 per hour</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="15 square meters">15 square meters</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Pine belongs to a tied lowest-price group" data-hint="One of the cheapest means Pine shares the extreme with at least one other room." data-fix="Pine is one of the cheapest rooms, so it belongs to a tied lowest-price group."><p class="quiz-question">6. Why does Clip 6 use <span class="ex">one of</span>?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine belongs to a tied lowest-price group">Pine belongs to a tied lowest-price group</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine is the only room in the building">Pine is the only room in the building</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine has no known price">Pine has no known price</button></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="thinking-card">
      <summary>Open the teacher script after checking</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than Maple.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Maple is the largest of the three rooms.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Pine is as quiet as Maple.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Pine is not as large as Cedar.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Cedar is fifteen dollars more expensive per hour than Pine.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Pine is one of the cheapest rooms in the building.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2>Read a recommendation from the evidence</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Room request: 30 people, a discussion activity, and a $60 hourly limit</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Pine is cheaper than Cedar, but it only has 24 chairs. Maple is the largest room, but at $70 per hour, it is more expensive than the client’s limit. Cedar has 32 chairs and costs $55 per hour. It is quieter than both Pine and Maple. For this group’s size, activity, and budget, Cedar is the best match of the three.</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You connected the recommendation to capacity, budget, and noise evidence.">
    <h3>Read, then choose the exact evidence</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Pine has too few chairs" data-hint="The request is for 30 people, but Pine has only 24 chairs." data-fix="Pine has too few chairs for the 30-person group."><p class="quiz-question">1. Why does the writer reject Pine?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine has too few chairs">Pine has too few chairs</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine is over the price limit">Pine is over the price limit</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Pine is larger than Maple">Pine is larger than Maple</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Maple costs more than $60 per hour" data-hint="The stated limit is $60, while Maple costs $70." data-fix="Maple costs more than the $60 hourly limit."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which criterion excludes Maple?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Maple costs more than $60 per hour">Maple costs more than $60 per hour</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Maple has fewer than 30 chairs">Maple has fewer than 30 chairs</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Maple is the quietest room">Maple is the quietest room</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Cedar meets the capacity, budget, and noise needs" data-hint="Cedar has 32 chairs, costs $55, and has the lowest noise value." data-fix="Cedar meets all three stated criteria."><p class="quiz-question">3. Why is Cedar the best match for this request?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Cedar meets the capacity, budget, and noise needs">Cedar meets the capacity, budget, and noise needs</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Cedar is the largest and cheapest room">Cedar is the largest and cheapest room</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Cedar has the fewest chairs">Cedar has the fewest chairs</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build the comparison and set</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="cedar is quieter than maple" data-hint="Begin with Cedar, add is and comparative quieter, then introduce Maple with than." data-fix="Cedar is quieter than Maple." data-success-message="Correct. The sentence locates Cedar relative to Maple on the noise scale.">
    <h3>Drill 3: build the reference comparison</h3><p class="tile-target">Cedar measures 30 dB; Maple measures 35 dB.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="maple">Maple</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="quieter">quieter</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="cedar">Cedar</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="is">is</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="than">than</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="maple is the largest of the three rooms" data-hint="Begin with Maple, use the largest, then define the complete set with of the three rooms." data-fix="Maple is the largest of the three rooms." data-success-message="Correct. The superlative identifies the area extreme inside a defined three-room set.">
    <h3>Drill 4: build the superlative set</h3><p class="tile-target">Maple has the greatest area in the three-room list.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="rooms">rooms</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="largest">largest</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="maple">Maple</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="of">of</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="is">is</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="three">three</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair three high-value errors</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="more" data-hint="Larger already contains the comparative marker -er." data-fix="Maple is larger than Cedar." data-why="Standard comparison uses one comparative marker, so more must be removed before larger." data-success-message="Correct. You kept one comparative marker and retained the degree relationship.">
    <h3>Drill 5: one comparative marker</h3><p>The room note says, “Maple is more larger than Cedar.” Tap the word to remove.</p>
    <div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="maple">Maple</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="is">is</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="more">more</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="larger">larger</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="cedar">than Cedar.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="quieter" data-hint="The as...as equality frame requires the base adjective quiet." data-fix="Pine is as quiet as Maple." data-why="As...as expresses equal degree around a base adjective, so quieter must become quiet." data-success-message="Correct. You restored the base adjective inside the equality frame.">
    <h3>Drill 6: equality uses the base adjective</h3><p>The data summary says, “Pine is as quieter as Maple.” Tap the word to repair.</p>
    <div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="pine">Pine</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="is">is</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="as-one">as</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="quieter">quieter</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="as-two">as Maple.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="the" data-hint="The possessive our already occupies the determiner position before largest room." data-fix="Maple is our largest room." data-why="Do not combine possessive our with article the in the same noun phrase." data-success-message="Correct. You let the possessive replace the superlative article.">
    <h3>Drill 7: article or possessive?</h3><p>The coordinator says, “Maple is our the largest room.” Tap the word to remove.</p>
    <div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="maple">Maple</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="is">is</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="our">our</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="the">the</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="largest">largest room.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform>
    <h3>Oral transform: change one comparison decision</h3>
    <p>Say every response before revealing it.</p>
    <div class="transform-list">
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> Cedar is quiet. Compare it with Maple from the table.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Cedar is quieter than Maple.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> Maple has the greatest area in the three-room set.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Maple is the largest of the three rooms.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Pine and Maple both measure 35 dB. Express equality.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Pine is as quiet as Maple.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> Pine has 30 m²; Cedar has 42 m². Use not as...as.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Pine is not as large as Cedar.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> Cedar costs $15 more per hour than Pine. State the exact degree.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Cedar is $15 more expensive per hour than Pine.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> Two rooms share the lowest price. Include Pine without claiming it is unique.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Pine is one of the cheapest rooms in the building.</strong></p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it: recommend a fictional event room</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real home, work, school, location, commute, purchases, finances, family, relationships, body, health, preferences, or living conditions. Use the fictional room data, invent neutral options, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Establish the evidence:</b> Compare all three rooms on area, price, noise, and capacity. Include one exact difference and one <span class="ex">as...as</span> statement.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Define the set:</b> Make three superlative claims, name the comparison set, and identify any claim that would change if a fourth room entered the list.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Change the client:</b> Recommend a room for a 20-person class with a $45 limit, then for a 35-person reception with no price limit. Explain why the best match changes with the criteria.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: give a 45–60 second room recommendation.</b> State the fictional client’s capacity, budget, and noise needs; use two comparatives, one measured difference, one equality or unequal-degree frame, and one superlative with a defined set; then justify one defensible recommendation from the table.</div>

  <h2>Final check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You controlled comparison logic, defined sets, form, spelling, equality, degree, possessives, and evidence-based recommendations.">
    <h3>Eight decisions from three training rooms</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="larger than Room A" data-hint="Room B has 45 m² and Room A has 32 m², so B is located relative to A on the area scale." data-fix="Room B is larger than Room A."><p class="quiz-question">1. Room A has 32 m²; Room B has 45 m². Room B is ___.</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="larger than Room A">larger than Room A</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the largest than Room A">the largest than Room A</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="more larger than Room A">more larger than Room A</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="the quietest of the three rooms" data-hint="Room C has the lowest noise value in the complete three-room set." data-fix="Room C is the quietest of the three rooms."><p class="quiz-question">2. Noise values are A 40 dB, B 35 dB, and C 28 dB. Room C is ___.</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the quietest of the three rooms">the quietest of the three rooms</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="quieter of one room">quieter of one room</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the more quiet than all">the more quiet than all</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="as expensive as" data-hint="Rooms A and C have the same $50 hourly price." data-fix="Room A is as expensive as Room C."><p class="quiz-question">3. Rooms A and C both cost $50 per hour. Room A is ___ Room C.</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="as expensive as">as expensive as</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="more expensive as">more expensive as</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="as more expensive as">as more expensive as</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="bigger" data-hint="Big has a short stressed vowel and final consonant in a one-syllable consonant-vowel-consonant spelling, so double g." data-fix="Room B is bigger than Room A."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which spelling correctly compares <span class="ex">big</span>?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="bigger">bigger</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="biger">biger</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="more bigger">more bigger</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="more useful" data-hint="Useful normally uses more for its comparative." data-fix="A room with a projector is more useful for this presentation."><p class="quiz-question">5. Room B has a projector; Room A does not. For this presentation, B is ___.</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="more useful">more useful</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="usefuller">usefuller</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="most useful than A">most useful than A</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="our best room" data-hint="The possessive our replaces the article before best room." data-fix="For recorded interviews, this is our best room."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which noun phrase correctly uses a possessive?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="our best room">our best room</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="our the best room">our the best room</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the our best room">the our best room</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="a little more expensive than" data-hint="Room B costs $52 and A costs $50, so the difference is small and the adjective uses more." data-fix="Room B is a little more expensive than Room A."><p class="quiz-question">7. Room B costs $52 and A costs $50 per hour. B is ___ A.</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="a little more expensive than">a little more expensive than</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="very expensiver than">very expensiver than</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the most expensive as">the most expensive as</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Room B meets the capacity and price limits" data-hint="The client needs 30 chairs and a price below $60; B has 34 chairs and costs $55." data-fix="Room B is the defensible recommendation because it meets both stated limits."><p class="quiz-question">8. A client needs 30 chairs for under $60. A has 24 chairs at $50; B has 34 at $55; C has 40 at $70. Why is B the best match?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Room B meets the capacity and price limits">Room B meets the capacity and price limits</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Room B is the cheapest and largest">Room B is the cheapest and largest</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Room B has fewer chairs than A">Room B has fewer chairs than A</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check quiz</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset quiz</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect and plan the next use</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Can you defend the comparison?</h3>
    <p>Explain the scale, reference, and set in <span class="ex">Cedar is quieter than Maple</span> and <span class="ex">Cedar is the quietest of the three rooms</span>. Then explain why <span class="ex">one of the cheapest</span> allows a tie and why <span class="ex">our largest room</span> has no <span class="ex">the</span>.</p>
    <p>Choose your next use: repeat the room recommendation with fewer notes, create a new three-option table, or complete the retrieval below tomorrow.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">Return tomorrow before rereading. Complete the <a href="#next-day-retrieval">five-minute next-day retrieval</a>, then revisit only the scale, set, form, spelling, equality, degree, or article pattern that still feels slow.</p>

  <section id="next-day-retrieval" data-lesson-extension="Five-minute next-day scale, set, equality, and spelling retrieval">
    <h2>Next-day retrieval</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a relative comparison, a defined superlative set, and an equality frame.">
      <h3>Choose from memory before looking back</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Next-day comparison choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quieter than">quieter than</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="more quieter than">more quieter than</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the largest of the three">the largest of the three</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the larger than the three">the larger than the three</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="as quiet as">as quiet as</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="as quieter as">as quieter as</button></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Cedar measures 30 dB and Maple 35 dB: Cedar is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quieter than" data-hint="Use one comparative marker and introduce the reference with than." data-fix="Cedar is quieter than Maple." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Maple.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Maple has the greatest area in the complete three-room list: It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the largest of the three" data-hint="Use the superlative and define its set with of the three." data-fix="Maple is the largest of the three." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
